{"content":{"result":[{"rank_order":1,"title":"How do I improve Amazon Bedrock performance and response times when I process and retrieve large scale data?","context":"### Use inference parameters\n\nUse [inference parameters](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/bedrock/latest/userguide/inference-parameters.html) to control and tune response generation. Reduce maxTokens to limit output length, as fewer output tokens require less computation. Use temperature, Top K, and Top P to control randomness and diversity. Use stop sequences to end generation early when a specific pattern is matched","url":"https://repost.aws/knowledge-center/bedrock-improve-performance-latency"},{"rank_order":2,"title":"CBRE and AWS perform natural language queries of structured data using Amazon Bedrock | Artificial Intelligence","context":"### Inference parameters\n\nRefer to Inference parameters for foundation models for more information on model inference parameters to influence the response generated by the model. We’ve used the following parameters specific to different inference steps to control maximum tokens to sample, randomness, probability distribution, and cutoff based on the sum of probabilities of the potential choices.\n\nThe following parameters specify to get relevant tables and output a SQL-to-English response:\n\n```\ninf_var_table = {\n \"max_tokens_to_sample\": 4096,\n \"temperature\": 1,\n \"top_k\": 250,\n \"top_p\": 0.999,\n \"stop_sequences\": [\"\\n\\nHuman\"],\n }\n```\n\nThe following parameters generate the SQL query:\n\n```\ninf_var_sql = {\n \"max_tokens_to_sample\": 4096,\n \"temperature\": 0.3,\n \"top_k\": 250,\n \"top_p\": 0.3,\n \"stop_sequences\": [\"\\n\\nHuman\"],\n }\n```","url":"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/machine-learning/cbre-and-aws-perform-natural-language-queries-of-structured-data-using-amazon-bedrock/"},{"rank_order":3,"title":"Class: Aws::BedrockRuntime::Types::InferenceConfiguration\n \n — AWS SDK for Ruby V3","context":"### #**top_p** ⇒ Float\n\nThe percentage of most-likely candidates that the model considers\nfor the next token. For example, if you choose a value of 0.8 for\n`topP`, the model selects from the top 80% of the probability\ndistribution of tokens that could be next in the sequence.\n\nThe default value is the default value for the model that you are\nusing. For more information, see Inference parameters for\nfoundation models.\n\nReturns:\n\n* (Float)\n\n| | |\n| --- | --- |\n| ``` 3641 3642 3643 3644 3645 3646 3647 3648 ``` | ``` # File 'gems/aws-sdk-bedrockruntime/lib/aws-sdk-bedrockruntime/types.rb', line 3641 class InferenceConfiguration < Struct.new( :max_tokens, :temperature, :top_p, :stop_sequences) SENSITIVE = [] include Aws::Structure end ``` |\n\nGenerated on Fri Nov 20 20:52:39 2026 by\nyard\n0.9.45 (ruby-3.4.3)","url":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/sdk-for-ruby/v3/api/Aws/BedrockRuntime/Types/InferenceConfiguration.html"}]}}

